  "question": {
    "text": "An unknown alochol is treated with the \"Lucas reagent\" to determine whether the alcohol is primary, secondary or tertiary. Which alcohol reacts fastest and by what mechanism :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "MDCxF",
      "text": "secondary alcohol by $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "eEG9D",
      "text": "tertiary alcohol by $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "6h1xi",
      "text": "secondary alcohol by $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "CpvRQ",
      "text": "tertiary alcohol by $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The reaction of alcohol with Lucas reagent is mostly an $\\mathrm{S}_{N}1$ mechanism reaction and the rate of reaction is directly proportional to the carbocation stability formed in the reaction, hence tertiary alcohol will react fastest.",
    "image": null
  },
